        {
            "id": "5",
            "cell_type": "markdown",
            "metadata": {
            },
            "source": [
                "\n",
                "We discuss the individual functionallities of the function `sem_groebner_basis`. Fist, Groebner basis computation is an elimination procedure to reduce a finite set of polynomials over a ring.\n",
                "\n",
                "1.  The parameter polynomial ring `P` over the rationals covers the entries of $ \\Lambda $ and $ \\Omega $. Similarly, the model polynomial ring `S` over the rationals represents the covariance matrix entries. To represent the equations $ X = \\Lambda^\\top X  + \\varepsilon $, we must define a ring `R` on the joint generators. \n",
                "2.  There exist canonical homomorphisms of the polynomial rings `P` and `S` to the joint ring `R` with which the design and covariance matrix can be mapped. This enables us to define the characterizing moment equation. Its vanishing ideal, i.e. the polynomial null-set, characterize all parameter configurations.\n",
                "2.  The Groebner basis computation iteratively reduces the equation system according to the given ordering. We say that a parameter is identified, if it is a unique function of the covariance matrix and of already identified parameters.\n",
                "\n",
                "In the next code chunk, we employ the Groebner basis computation on the path $$ X_1 \\longrightarrow X_2 \\longrightarrow X_3 .$$"
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "6",
            "cell_type": "code",
            "metadata": {
            },
            "source": [
                "U = gaussian_graphical_model(graph_from_edges(Directed, [[1,2],[2,3]]));\n",
                "G, R, Rvars, I, o = sem_groebner_basis(U);\n",
                "G"
            ],
            "outputs": [
                {
                    "output_type": "execute_result",
                    "execution_count": 1,
                    "data": {
                        "text/plain": "Gröbner basis with elements\n  1: s[1, 3]*s[2, 2] - s[1, 2]*s[2, 3]\n  2: w[3]*s[2, 2] + s[2, 3]^2 - s[2, 2]*s[3, 3]\n  3: w[3]*s[1, 2] + s[1, 3]*s[2, 3] - s[1, 2]*s[3, 3]\n  4: w[2]*s[1, 1] + s[1, 2]^2 - s[1, 1]*s[2, 2]\n  5: w[1] - s[1, 1]\n  6: l[2, 3]*s[2, 3] + w[3] - s[3, 3]\n  7: l[2, 3]*s[2, 2] - s[2, 3]\n  8: l[2, 3]*s[1, 2] - s[1, 3]\n  9: l[1, 2]*s[1, 2] + w[2] - s[2, 2]\n  10: l[1, 2]*s[1, 1] - s[1, 2]\n  11: w[2]*w[3] - l[1, 2]*s[1, 3]*s[2, 3] + l[2, 3]*s[2, 2]*s[2, 3] - w[2]*s[3, 3]\n  12: l[2, 3]*w[2] + l[1, 2]*s[1, 3] - l[2, 3]*s[2, 2]\nwith respect to the ordering\n  degrevlex([l[1, 2], l[2, 3], w[1], w[2], w[3]])*degrevlex([s[1, 1], s[1, 2], s[1, 3], s[2, 2], s[2, 3], s[3, 3]])"
                    },
                    "metadata": {
                    }
                }
            ],
            "execution_count": 1
